---
description: Enforce the six-phase product lifecycle — no phase work before the previous phase is approved
alwaysApply: true
---

## What This Rule Does

`01-specify-rules.mdc` gates one **feature**. This rule gates the whole
**product**. It ensures nobody designs a system nobody specified, and nobody
builds a system nobody designed.

The two are nested, not competing:

```
PRODUCT LIFECYCLE  (this rule)      once per product
  1 REQUIREMENTS -> 2 ANALYSIS -> 3 DESIGN -> 4 DEVELOPMENT <-> 5 TESTING -> 6 PRODUCTION
                                                 |
                            FEATURE PIPELINE (01-specify-rules), once per feature
                            analyze -> clarify -> constitution -> plan -> specify
                                    -> implement -> checklist -> commit
```

Phases 4 and 5 are a loop, not two steps. A failing test returns work to
development; the lifecycle stays in `TESTING`.

---

## The State File Is The Truth

`lifecycle/state.json` records which phase the product is in and which gates a
human approved. It is machine-owned — `guard-write.mjs` blocks hand-edits.

```bash
node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/tools/lifecycle.mjs product                   # start here: one-screen briefing
node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/tools/lifecycle.mjs status                    # where are we, and is it still true
node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/tools/lifecycle.mjs check [PHASE]             # consent 1: artifacts exist
node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/tools/lifecycle.mjs gate PHASE                # who may judge it, and why
node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/tools/lifecycle.mjs record-gate PHASE --verdict GO --by "<the gate's reviewer>"
                                                           # consent 2: the review verdict
node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/tools/lifecycle.mjs approve PHASE --by "name"  # consent 3: the human
node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/tools/lifecycle.mjs advance
node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/tools/lifecycle.mjs rollback PHASE --reason "..."
```

## Three Consents, All Required

`approve` refuses unless all three are present, the phase is the current one,
and the previous phase is cleared:

| Consent | Recorded by | Proves |
|---|---|---|
| mechanical | `approve`, recomputed live | the artifacts exist and are not templates |
| judgement | `/lifecycle-gate` via `record-gate` | they are worth building on |
| human | `approve --by "<name>"` | someone is accountable |

The three must be three **parties**, not one party three times:

- The reviewer is named in the gate file and is never one of the phase's authors.
  `record-gate` refuses a verdict filed under any other role.
- `approve` refuses a signature from the same name that recorded the verdict.
- The verdict is bound to the artifact hashes the reviewer actually read. Edit a
  document between the review and the signature and the signature is refused —
  the GO was given on an older draft.

| Gate | Authored by | Judged by | Because |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 Requirements | `product-manager`, `ux-bridge` | `business-analyst` | phase 2 inherits every ambiguity left in them |
| 2 Analysis | `business-analyst` | `solution-architect` | it inherits the model whole |
| 3 Design | `solution-architect`, `ux-bridge` | `security-auditor` | an architect defending a choice is not reviewing it |
| 4 Development | the speckit pipeline | `test-engineer` | whoever must test it should say when it is finished |
| 5 Testing | `test-engineer` | `product-manager` | only the author of the acceptance criteria knows what they meant |
| 6 Production | `ops-reviewer` | `security-auditor` | a runbook's author cannot judge whether a stranger can follow it |

None of this proves a reviewer read anything, and nothing in a repo can. It
makes an author judging their own work something you have to do on purpose,
rather than the default that it was.

## Status Is Derived, Not Stored

The state file holds evidence; the status is computed on every read.

| Status | Means |
|---|---|
| `NOT_STARTED` / `IN_PROGRESS` | no approval yet |
| `APPROVED` | all three consents, all still valid |
| `INHERITED` | brownfield; done informally before the lifecycle was adopted |
| `STALE` | **was** approved, and something it was approved against changed |
| `BLOCKED` | an earlier phase is not cleared |

`STALE` is the one that matters. Approval hashes every required artifact and
stamps the gate definition's version. Edit an approved design document, or
tighten a gate criterion, and the phase is `STALE` on the very next command —
and `guard-phase.mjs` starts blocking source writes again.

When a phase reads `STALE`, do not offer to re-approve it. Offer to re-review it:
the approval that lapsed was granted against text that no longer exists.

The `SessionStart` hook injects the current phase automatically. If a repo has
no `lifecycle/state.json` it never adopted the lifecycle, and this rule is
inert — that is a valid configuration, not a violation.

